# Resume a saved map: load it into pgo_node and keep mapping in its frame.
#
# Served by pgo_node (`syncai_mapping`, in SyncAI-Robot-Workspace) on the
# relative name `resume_mapping`, so it lands on <robot_id>/pgo/resume_mapping.
# Added 2026-11. Until then a run could only begin empty (StartMapping), so a
# map missing one room was mapped again from its first metre.
#
# What it does, in order: read the saved keyframes (poses.txt + patches/) from
# map_path, pause intake, reset the LIO front end over ResetLIO (reset_lio
# true), collect about a second of scans, find where the robot stands in the
# saved map, then go MAPPING with the saved keyframes loaded and the new
# odometry placed in their frame. The saved map is published at once, so the
# console shows it before the first new keyframe.
#
# THE SAVED KEYFRAMES ARE FROZEN. They are held as constants, never optimised,
# so the map frame is exactly the saved map's: waypoints, keepout.json and
# octomap_edits.json made in it stay valid. New keyframes are tied to them by
# loop closures; the saved map's own drift is not corrected by the new data.
#
# Finding the robot, three ways (the response's method says which one did):
#  - use_hint: registration is tried only near the hint, and a hint that does
#    not register fails the call -- it never falls back to the search, because
#    a hint that is wrong usually means the operator and the robot disagree
#    about where it is.
#  - no hint, and the nav session's localizer last saved a pose on THIS map
#    (config/state/<robot_id>/last_pose.json, compared by the map directory's
#    name): that pose is tried first, within about a metre of it. It is where
#    the robot stopped navigating, so "navigate, switch to mapping, resume"
#    needs no pose from the caller. A robot moved since then does not register
#    there, and the call goes on to the search; it does not fail.
#  - otherwise the saved keyframe positions are searched over a yaw sweep
#    (seconds); a place that looks like another one (a second candidate
#    scoring about as well, elsewhere) is refused as ambiguous rather than
#    guessed.
#
# Only IDLE is accepted, as for StartMapping. A failure leaves pgo IDLE with
# nothing loaded; when it fails after the LIO reset (the search found no
# pose), the odometry has been reset all the same, which is harmless in IDLE.
# The call BLOCKS for the load, the reset, the scans and the search: tens of
# seconds on a large map. Size the client's timeout for it (60 s).
#
# save_maps then writes every keyframe, saved and new, with their indices in
# one sequence and a sessions.json beside poses.txt naming where each run
# begins. It must be given ANOTHER directory than map_path: a save into the
# directory the run was resumed from is refused, since it would delete the
# only copy of the saved map before the new one is whole.
#
# THE ROBOT MUST BE STANDING STILL, inside the saved map, from the call until
# it returns. See ResetLIO.srv.

# The saved map's directory: poses.txt and patches/, as save_maps wrote them
# with save_patches. A map saved without patches cannot be resumed.
string map_path

# Reset the LIO front end first -- what the console sends. False resumes over
# the running odometry stream, a bag-replay affordance (see StartMapping.srv).
bool reset_lio

# Where the robot is in the saved map, roughly: the lidar body's position (m)
# and heading (rad) in the map frame. Within a few metres and about 45 degrees
# is enough. z only picks the storey (the saved keyframes within 1.5 m of it).
# Ignored unless use_hint.
bool use_hint
float64 x
float64 y
float64 z
float64 yaw
---
bool success
# Rendered verbatim by the operator console, as in StartMapping.srv.
string message

# As in StartMapping.srv. 0.0 when the call failed before the LIO reset.
float64 lio_last_odom_time

# Keyframes loaded from map_path. 0 when the load failed.
uint32 prior_key_poses

# How the robot was found. METHOD_NONE when it was not. METHOD_LAST_POSE
# (2026-11): near the pose the localizer last saved, see above; a consumer
# that names the values must not assume there are three.
uint8 METHOD_NONE=0
uint8 METHOD_HINT=1
uint8 METHOD_GLOBAL=2
uint8 METHOD_LAST_POSE=3
uint8 method

# The lidar body's pose in the saved map's frame, as registered. Zero when
# the call failed.
float64 aligned_x
float64 aligned_y
float64 aligned_z
float64 aligned_yaw

# The registration behind it, for the log and for calibrating the gates:
# mean squared distance over the overlap (m^2), the share of the scan the
# saved map covers, the share of that overlap within the inlier distance, and
# the smallest / largest eigenvalue of the registration's x / y information
# (near 0 in a straight corridor, where the pose can slide along it).
float64 fitness
float64 overlap_ratio
float64 inlier_ratio
float64 xy_info_ratio

# Roll / pitch (deg) between the two runs' up directions, as registered. Each
# run levels itself by its own static IMU initialisation, and two of those
# differ by a fraction of a degree (0.76 measured on robot01 between two runs
# begun 85 deg apart in heading). The alignment KEEPS it: the new run takes the
# saved map's up, or it would lean on the saved map by a centimetre a metre.
# (Until 2026-11-20 it was dropped, and refused above 1 deg.) Above pgo's
# resume_max_tilt_deg (3) the call is refused as a registration gone wrong.
float64 tilt_residual_deg
